- `public/js/panels/plans.js` — the trade-off warning line on a plan card.
- `prisma/seed/tenants.ts` — GOLD ₹2,500, SILVER ₹800, highway double.
- `tests/contracts.test.ts` — 28 tests, the four the phase names among them.

### Decisions

**Decision** The contract term enters the cost function as a NEGATIVE number —
`-(1 - P(breach)) x penalty`, the penalty this dispatch avoids — rather than as
the positive `P(breach) x penalty` the build plan writes.
**Why** The assignment minimises the sum over a plan, and a job that goes
unserved contributes `UNAVAILABLE_COST`, a flat sentinel identical for every job.
So a positive exposure on the served cell makes a GOLD job the EXPENSIVE job: with
one van left the solver hands it to the consumer and lets the contract breach, and
raising the weight makes that worse rather than better. The only place the cost of
NOT serving a job can enter is by shifting the served cells down by what a
dispatch avoids.
**Alternative** Put the penalty on the unassigned side instead — give each job's
padding column its own penalty rather than the shared sentinel.
**Why not** `UNAVAILABLE_COST` means "there is nothing here to choose", not "this
is very bad", and allocation.ts's own comment is the argument that it is not a
disguised constraint. Making it per-job re-opens exactly that question, in the
file where invariant 3 is hardest to defend.
**Tradeoff** A cost term that is never positive reads oddly, and `plan.totalCost`
can now be negative. Both are commented where they appear.

**Decision** Safety became a lexicographic ordering in the GLOBAL ASSIGNMENT, not
only inside one job's ranking: `buildPlan` runs one Hungarian per safety level,
HIGH_RISK first, each band seeing only the units the bands above it left.
**Why** `ranking.ts` has held invariant 4 since Phase 8 for "who do we send to
THIS job". It says nothing about "which job gets the last van", and that gap was
unreachable in practice only because nothing in the cost function was large enough
to exploit it. A ₹2,500 penalty at the BALANCED weight is about a hundred points
against option totals in the forties. The phase's own required test — a HIGH_RISK
consumer beating a GOLD contract job — cannot pass honestly without this.
**Alternative** A very large safety bonus inside the cost function.
**Why not** That is precisely the mistake invariant 4 exists to rule out; a
weighted term can always be outvoted, and the phase that introduced a hundred-point
term is the phase that proves it.
**Tradeoff** The plan is no longer globally cost-optimal. Sequential bands are
greedy ACROSS bands and exact WITHIN each, so a HIGH_RISK job can take a unit an
ELEVATED job needed more, at a higher total. Stated in the file. It only bites
when two safety levels genuinely compete; the whole evaluation harness (every job
NORMAL) produced byte-identical outcome metrics before and after.

**Decision** `PROTECT_CONTRACTS` is BALANCED with one weight changed —
`contractSlaExposure` x10 — and nothing else.
**Why** Plan B and plan C are then comparable: the only difference between them
is how much a contract promise is worth, which is the question a dispatcher is
actually being asked. A vector that also moved travel and operational weights
would be a different opinion about everything, and "why is C different?" would
have no short answer.
**Alternative** Tune all six weights for a contract-first posture.
**Why not** Nothing has been measured that would justify any of the other five
numbers, and an untuned guess dressed as a policy is worse than one honest dial.
**Tradeoff** There are now four plan cards where the UI was designed for three.
Bootstrap wraps them; the count lives in `WEIGHT_VECTORS` and nowhere else.

**Decision** Cross-tenant contention ESCALATES rather than resolving — an APPROVE
`ActionRequest` carrying both jobs, both costs, and the contested unit.
**Why** At the same safety level the objective will always prefer the job with a
priced obligation attached, and "we sent the van to the fleet because the fleet
pays us" is not a sentence anybody wants to discover after the fact.
**Alternative** Let the cost function settle it silently; it already can.
**Why not** The build plan is explicit and it is right: a system that silently
chooses which paying customer to disappoint is a system nobody deploys.
**Tradeoff** APPROVE blocks. A dispatcher who never answers leaves one of two
people waiting — which is the honest shape of the situation, because there was
only ever one van.

**Decision** The card is raised on the job that LOST, once per job, skipped while
a PENDING card of that kind already exists.
**Why** The loser is the job whose fate is open, and it is the job somebody will
ring up about. The debounced zone re-plan fires every couple of seconds under
load and would otherwise stack an identical card until somebody answered.
**Alternative** Key idempotency on the solver run.
**Why not** Every run is a new run, so that is not idempotency, it is a new card
per run with extra steps.
**Tradeoff** If a dispatcher rejects a card and the contention persists, the next
solve raises a fresh one. Correct, but it means "rejected" does not mean "stop
asking".

**Decision** `TENANT_CONTRACT_EXCLUDES` stays a stub after the phase that was
supposed to implement it.
**Why** The obvious candidate is not one. `covered_services.covered = false`
means "this contract does not pay for this service", and
`src/domain/settlementPath.ts` already resolves that: the driver is a WALK_IN for
that job and is served and billed accordingly. Turning cover into a hard filter
would make the system refuse to help a stranded person over a billing question —
the same mistake the SLA filter had to be talked out of at Phase 10. No contract
term in the schema names which providers may serve it, so nothing here can be
honest yet.
**Alternative** Implement it against the coverage flag anyway, so the code has no
stub in it.
**Why not** Documenting as implemented what is not implemented is the one thing
the standing instruction forbids outright, and a filter that fires for the wrong
reason is worse than a filter that does not fire.
**Tradeoff** One of the ten reason codes is still dead. Recorded in §15.

**Decision** A vehicle is checked against two tenancy conflicts at registration:
the plate already on file under another tenant, and the vehicle's tenant
differing from its driver's.
**Why** The tenant on the vehicle decides whose contract a breakdown is served
under, whose SLA window the solver prices, and who is invoiced. Two answers to
those means the system picks whichever row came back first — serving one fleet's
vehicle on another fleet's contract, in a job that looks entirely ordinary.
**Alternative** Rely on the unique index on `vehicles.registration`.
**Why not** It catches the first conflict and produces "unique constraint
failed" rather than a sentence naming the tenant that holds the plate, and it
catches the second not at all — that row inserts cleanly.
**Tradeoff** The read-then-insert is not atomic; the index is still what enforces
it, and the loser of a race gets a 409 from the error middleware.

### Concurrency

**Scenario** Two dispatchers, or a dispatcher and the debounced zone re-plan,
solve the same zone at the same moment and both see the same contested unit.
**What races** Both call `raiseContentionApprovals` for the same losing job.
**Why dangerous** Two identical APPROVE cards for one decision; approving one
leaves the other pending, and the queue shows a question that has been answered.
**How handled** A `findFirst` for a PENDING card of that kind on that job, then
the insert. This is a read-then-write and it is NOT atomic — two callers inside
the same window can both find nothing and both insert.
**Assumption relied on** That the window is small (both statements are on the
same connection, milliseconds apart) and that the failure is cosmetic rather than
harmful: the second card is a duplicate question, not a duplicate side effect,
because approving one of these executes nothing (`src/api/actions.ts` records the
decision and stops). A unique partial index on
`(job_id, kind) WHERE status = 'PENDING'` would close it and is the right fix if
duplicates ever show up; it is not there yet, and §15 says so rather than the
comment claiming a guarantee the code does not make.

**Scenario** A contract is edited — re-tiered, deactivated, its window shortened
— while a technician is driving to a job under it.
**What races** The solver re-plans mid-incident and reads the contract as it is
NOW, while the customer was quoted the terms as they WERE.
**Why dangerous** The objective changes underneath a live job, producing a
re-plan nobody can explain, and in the other direction a customer is served under
terms they were never offered.
**How handled** Not by locking. `jobs.sla_policy_id` is resolved once inside the
intake transaction and never re-read — the third pinned column alongside
`price_list_id` and `settlement_path` — and `buildDispatchSnapshot` follows the
pinned policy rather than looking up the active contract.
**Assumption relied on** That an SLA policy row is not itself edited in place.
Pinning the id fixes WHICH policy; it does not freeze that policy's numbers. A
`UPDATE sla_policies SET response_minutes` would still reach live jobs. Contract
terms are not edited in this system today, and a versioned policy row is the fix
if they ever are.

### Weaknesses introduced
- **The contention guard is read-then-write.** Two concurrent solves can raise
  two identical APPROVE cards. Cosmetic at current scale — the action executes
  nothing — and the fix is one partial unique index. Matters only when two
  dispatchers work the same zone during a surge, which is exactly when it would
  happen.
- **Pinning the policy id does not pin the policy's numbers.** Editing an
  `sla_policies` row reaches every live job that pinned it. Nothing edits them
  today. Does not matter at current scale; would matter the first time a contract
  is renegotiated in place.
- **`breachProbability` is a ruler, not a model.** Linear from a hand-set
  half-window threshold to certainty at the deadline. Nothing about travel-time
  error has ever been measured in this project, so the shape is asserted, not
  fitted. It is monotone in arrival time, which is the only property the ranking
  actually depends on.
- **The contract credit is large.** At BALANCED a GOLD penalty is worth about a
  hundred points against option totals in the forties, so a contract job usually
  does win a contested van. It is still a weight — an option a hundred points
  cheaper beats it — but "usually decisive" is close enough to the constraint
  invariant 3 forbids that it is worth saying out loud rather than discovering.
- **Safety bands cost optimality.** The plan is exact within a band and greedy
  across bands. A HIGH_RISK job can take a unit an ELEVATED job needed more.
  Deliberate; invariant 4 is the point.
- **`TENANT_CONTRACT_EXCLUDES` is still a stub.** Ten reason codes, nine real.
- **`POST /vehicles` is the only vehicle route.** No update, no deactivation, no
  transfer between tenants — and transfer is the operation a real fleet actually
  performs when it sells a van. Registration was what the phase required; the rest
  is not built and is not pretended.
- **Nothing reads `ContractTerms.tier`.** The penalty carries all the weight; the
  tier rides along for the escalation card and the UI. `contract_tiers.priority`
  is still unused by any decision.

### Concepts now genuinely in use
Lexicographic ordering applied to a MATCHING rather than to a sort — safety bands
solved in sequence, each seeing what the band above left. The baseline problem in
a linear objective: a term that prices an alternative the model cannot represent
has to be folded into the cells as a credit, and getting the sign wrong inverts
the behaviour it was added for. Pinned terms as a concurrency technique — three
columns now (`price_list_id`, `settlement_path`, `sla_policy_id`) that exist so a
later read cannot disagree with an earlier promise. Escalation as a first-class
outcome: a decision the system can make and deliberately does not.

### Interview angle
"You put a contract penalty in the cost function. Which way does the sign go, and
why?" Negative — the term is what the dispatch AVOIDS, not what it risks, because
the assignment prices an unserved job with one flat sentinel and the only way the
penalty for going unserved can enter is as a credit on the served cells. Priced
the other way round, a GOLD job becomes the expensive job and the last van goes to
the consumer while the contract breaches.

"Safety outranks contract tier — where is that actually enforced?" In two places
for two different questions: `ranking.ts` for which crew goes to one job, and the
safety bands in `buildPlan` for which job gets the last van. The second arrived
this phase, because a hundred-point contract term is the first thing in the cost
function large enough to have exploited the gap.

### What broke
**The cost term was backwards the first time it was wired.** Written as the build
plan states it — `P(breach) x penalty`, a positive cost — it made the GOLD job the
expensive cell in the matrix, so with one van and two jobs the Hungarian gave the
van to the consumer and left the contract breaching, and `PROTECT_CONTRACTS` made
it worse in proportion to its weight. Caught while designing the phase's own
required test, before the code ran: writing down what the assertion should say
("the GOLD job wins when both are NORMAL") made the sign obviously wrong. The fix
is the credit formulation above and `tests/contracts.test.ts` pins it with an
assertion whose comment says why.

**The phase's HIGH_RISK-beats-GOLD test could not have passed.** `buildPlan` fed
one Hungarian a matrix of cost totals and consulted safety nowhere, so invariant 4
had no representation in the global assignment at all. It had been invisible for
eleven phases because no term was ever big enough to notice. Fixed with safety
bands; the evaluation harness's outcome metrics are byte-identical before and
after, which is the evidence that the change only fires when two safety levels
compete.

**`jobs.sla_policy_id` had never been written.** Three consumers read it —
`buildDispatchSnapshot`, `slaEscalation`, the console — and intake had never set
it, so `slaResponseMinutes` was null on every job in production and the SLA
filter, the SLA cost term and the SLA escalation sweep were all dead paths that
only fixtures setting the column by hand ever exercised. Found while looking for
where to hang the contract terms.

**The test fixture could not be torn down.** `job_events` rejects DELETE, so
deleting a job fails on the cascade, and with the jobs still there the driver,
vehicle and tenant behind them cannot go either — the gotcha the file already
warned about, met from the other side. The suite passed 28/28 and failed in
`afterAll`. Rewritten as an idempotent upsert fixture whose only cleanup is
cancelling the jobs (a terminal status is what keeps them out of other suites'
snapshots) and deleting the APPROVE cards (the contention test asserts that the
first call raises one and the second does not, so a card left pending from the
last run would make the first call a no-op).

**The fairness grep went red.** `tests/evaluation.test.ts` reads `src/engine/cost.ts`
and asserts it never matches `/fairness|gini|earnings/i`. The new term's comment
explained why a contract obligation belongs in the objective by contrasting it
with the earnings Gini — and named it. Reworded rather than weakening the test,
which was doing exactly its job.

**A long heredoc through the Bash tool failed with `unexpected EOF`, twice,
writing nothing** — the same failure Phase 19 recorded, at around the same length.
Both files went through the Write tool instead. The note from last phase was
right and is now confirmed rather than suspected.

## Phase 21 — Fleet dashboard / 2026-09-02 / phase 21: fleet dashboard

### Built
No migration — every number reads rows Phases 1-20 already write.

- `src/domain/fleetSlaOutcome.ts` — `slaOutcomeFor()`: MET / BREACHED /
  UNDECIDED / NOT_APPLICABLE for one incident. The fourth state is the point —
  a job still inside its window is excluded from both halves of "SLA met %",
  not counted as either.
- `src/domain/fleetMetrics.ts` — `slaMetSummary()`, `currentExposurePaise()`,
  `meanResolutionSummary()`, `spendPaiseThisMonth()`, `isActive()`,
  `startOfMonthUtc()`. Pure; all five take the same plain `Incident[]`.
- `src/domain/repeatPattern.ts` — `repeatPatternFlag()`. A count over a
  60-day window, threshold 3, labelled a RULE in its own header and on the
  card. No model.
- `src/domain/fleetPrior.ts` — `fleetVehiclePrior()`. Folds up to
  `MAX_PRIOR_INCIDENTS` (5) past faults into a starting belief via the SAME
  `applyLikelihood` symptom evidence already uses.
- `src/domain/faultRules.ts` — `classifySymptoms()` gains an optional second
  parameter, `startingPrior`, defaulting to `FAULT_PRIORS`. Every existing
  caller is unaffected; one new caller passes a sharpened prior.
- `src/commit/fleetIncidents.ts` — `fleetIncidentsFor()`, the one query
  everything else reads. Explicit `tenantId` filter, not the auth-context
  extension — this file is called from a route that already has the caller's
  own tenant off their token.
- `src/commit/fleetPrior.ts` — `fleetVehiclePriorFaults()`: FLEET-tenant
  vehicles only, most-recent incidents first, via the job's latest
  `FaultHypothesis.topFault`.
- `src/commit/fleetDashboard.ts` — `buildFleetDashboard()`, the one function
  the route calls. Groups incidents by vehicle, flags the worst pattern per
  vehicle, sorts flagged vehicles first.
- `src/intake/createJob.ts` — reads `fleetVehiclePriorFaults` and folds it
  through `fleetVehiclePrior` before `classifySymptoms`, inside the intake
  transaction.
- `src/api/fleet.ts` — `GET /fleet/dashboard`. Tenant from the token, never a
  query parameter. Money rendered once, server-side, through
  `formatRupees`.
- `src/auth/permissions.ts` — new resource `fleetDashboard`, FLEET_MANAGER
  read-only.
- `src/workers/notification.ts` — `notifyFleetManagerOfStateChange()`,
  `FLEET_STATE_CHANGE_COALESCE_MS` (10 minutes, ~6.7x the driver's 90
  seconds), `deliverFleetStateChangeNotification()`. Routed inside the
  existing `processNotification` by the queued job's own `name`
  (`'fleet-state-change'` vs `'driver-replan'`) rather than a second queue.
- `src/realtime/publish.ts` — `publishFleetNotification()`, to the
  `tenant:<id>` room a FLEET_MANAGER already stands in
  (`rooms.ts`'s `standingRoomsFor`, unread by anything until now).
- `src/commit/redispatch.ts` — the one existing driver-notification call site
  gained a sibling call. No new trigger points invented.
- `public/fleet.html`, `public/js/pages/fleet.js`,
  `public/js/panels/fleetDashboard.js`, `state.js`/`api.js` additions.
- Tests: `tests/fleetDomain.test.ts` (26, pure), `tests/fleetDashboard.test.ts`
  (12, real Postgres — includes the phase's own named check), 
  `tests/fleetNotifications.test.ts` (6, real Postgres).

### Decisions

**Decision** SLA outcome is a FOUR-state answer (`fleetSlaOutcome.ts`), not a
boolean.
**Why** A job raised ten minutes ago, still inside its window, is neither a
success nor a failure — it has not happened yet. Counting it either way makes
"SLA met %" swing on jobs still in progress. UNDECIDED lets the denominator be
DECIDED jobs only, the same move `evaluation/metrics.ts` makes measuring
resolution time only over jobs that finished.
**Alternative** Two states — met or not-yet-breached counts as "on track", roll
it into the percentage now.
**Why not** That is optimistic rounding dressed as a number: a job with nine
minutes left on a thirty-minute window is not "on track" in any sense a fleet
manager should trust, and the honest answer is "not decided yet", not "fine so
far".
**Tradeoff** The percentage can be computed over very few decided jobs early
in a slow month and read as noisy. `slaDecidedCount` rides beside it on the
dashboard so a fleet manager can see the sample size, not just the number.

**Decision** Current exposure is a BOOLEAN "already breached" summed over
active incidents, never a probability.
**Why** Phase 20's `contractSlaExposure` prices a DISPATCH DECISION under
uncertainty about an option's estimated arrival — an input this dashboard does
not have (no candidate is being scored) and should not manufacture from a
stale ETA row. What the dashboard CAN say honestly is simpler: has this
incident's window already closed with nobody there, using the identical
deadline arithmetic `feasibility.ts` and `slaEscalation.ts` already apply.
**Alternative** Reuse `avoidedBreachPenaltyPaise` against the job's latest
`EtaEstimate` row.
**Why not** An ETA row can be stale or entirely absent (a job not yet
assigned a unit has none), and turning "we do not know" into a probability
would be inventing precision the data does not support — the same objection
Phase 19's fail-closed stock rule answers for parts.
**Tradeoff** A job dispatched with a comfortable-looking ETA that is about to
miss its window shows zero exposure until the deadline actually passes, one
minute before it would under a probabilistic model. Simpler and never
overstates; understates by at most one decision cycle.

**Decision** "Spend this month" reads `Estimate.totalPaise` for jobs RESOLVED
this month (`src/commit/estimates.ts`'s own `approvedTotalFor`, re-derived
inline via a join rather than N+1 calls), not the `tenant:<id>` ledger
account balance.
**Why** The ledger booking for a FLEET_TENANT job with a CAPPED contract
coverage routes the uncovered remainder to the generic
`CUSTOMER_RECEIVABLE_ACCOUNT`, not the tenant's own account — a latent gap
from Phase 15c that happens not to bite today because every seeded fleet
contract is uncapped, but auditing and fixing that booking is out of scope for
this phase. Reading the estimate total sidesteps the question entirely and
still matches earnings.ts's own words for the number: "what the work was
worth".
**Alternative** Fix the Phase 15c booking gap first, then read the ledger.
**Why not** A phase named "fleet dashboard" fixing an unrelated Phase 15c
accounting bug is scope creep dressed as thoroughness. Flagged here and left
for whichever phase actually owns billing correctness.
**Tradeoff** If a fleet contract with a coverage cap is ever configured,
dashboard spend and the ledger's own tenant-account balance could disagree.
Recorded rather than silently risked.

**Decision** The safety-band precedent from Phase 20 (splitting a plan into
sequential Hungarian runs) has NO analogue here — the dashboard has no
allocation to protect.
**Why** Worth stating precisely: nothing on this dashboard is a dispatch
decision. Every number is a READ over what earlier phases already decided and
wrote. There is no ranking, no assignment, no place invariant 4 could be
violated by adding a metric.

**Decision** A vehicle's starting prior folds in past incidents through the
SAME `applyLikelihood` mechanism a symptom answer already goes through, capped
at the five most recent, rather than a bespoke weighting formula.
**Why** One update mechanism, already tested, already the thing a maintainer
reading `faultRules.ts` recognises. A second kind of "how much does this
observation move the belief" arithmetic living beside the first would be two
things to explain instead of one.
**Alternative** A frequency-weighted multiplier — each repeat multiplies that
fault's prior odds by a fixed factor.
**Why not** Nothing about Bayesian updating changes just because the
"observation" is history instead of a symptom; reusing `applyLikelihood`
means the fold-in is provably the same kind of belief revision as everything
around it, checkable by the same entropy maths.
**Tradeoff** The cap at five exists only to bound how sharp the prior can get
before a single symptom is even asked about — an unmeasured, hand-set number,
the same honest admission every other threshold in this project carries.

**Decision** Fleet notifications route through the SAME queue and the SAME
`processNotification` handler as driver notifications, distinguished by the
BullMQ job's own `name` field, rather than a second queue.
**Why** Both are "wait, then describe where the job settled" — identical
shape, different audience and window. A second queue would be a second
worker registration, a second idempotency namespace, and a second thing that
could silently stop being polled.
**Alternative** One `NotificationJobData.kind` field inside the payload
instead of routing on the queue job name.
**Why not** Either works; `job.name` was already the label `enqueueDebounced`
passes for the driver path, so using the field that already existed for
exactly this purpose was one fewer thing to add to the payload shape.

### Concurrency

**Scenario** A re-plan fires for a fleet job; the ten-minute fleet window and
the ninety-second driver window are both open at once.
**What races** Nothing — they are two independent debounce keys
(`job:<id>:replan` vs `job:<id>:fleet-state-change`) on two independent
`enqueueDebounced` calls. Two BullMQ jobs are created, each collapsing its
own bursts.
**Why this matters anyway** It is worth stating precisely BECAUSE the two
calls sit next to each other in `redispatch.ts` and could look like one
event fanning out to two recipients. They are two separate coalescing
windows that happen to start at the same trigger and settle independently —
if the fleet manager's ten-minute window still has three more re-plans to
absorb after the driver's ninety seconds have already delivered a message,
that is correct, not a bug.

**Scenario** A tenant has two FLEET_MANAGER users; a fleet state-change fires.
**What races** Nothing new — `Promise.all` over per-recipient
`notification.create` calls, each its own row, each independently
idempotent through the outer `runIdempotently` on the queue job id. A crash
mid-`Promise.all` after some rows are written and a redelivery both land on
the SAME idempotency key, so a partial write is replayed as a whole retry —
`runIdempotently`'s existing guarantee, unchanged by there being two
recipients instead of one.

### Weaknesses introduced
- **Fleet spend can drift from the ledger if a fleet contract ever gets a
  coverage cap.** Documented above. Not reachable with today's seed data.
- **`currentExposurePaise` understates risk by up to one decision cycle** for
  a job dispatched with an ETA that is about to miss its window — it reads
  zero until the deadline has actually passed. Deliberate; the alternative
  invents precision from a possibly stale or absent ETA row.
- **The repeat-pattern rule matches on the EXACT fault code**, not a category
  — `FLAT_BATTERY` and `FAILED_BATTERY` never combine into one pattern even
  though both are "the battery". Simpler and defensible without inventing a
  fault taxonomy this codebase does not otherwise have; a vehicle alternating
  between the two codes would go unflagged.
- **The intake prior has no decay beyond the five-incident cap.** A vehicle
  with two ancient battery incidents and one very recent tyre incident is
  still nudged toward "battery" by the two old ones — there is no recency
  WEIGHTING, only a recency CAP (the five most recent, unweighted equally
  whether one day or fifty-nine days old).
- **`GET /fleet/dashboard` has no pagination.** A fleet of thousands of
  vehicles would return one very large JSON body. Not reachable by the three
  seeded fleets; the seed's largest is under thirty vehicles.
- **"This month" is a UTC calendar month.** A fleet manager in Bengaluru can
  read a report that rolled over at 5:30am local time rather than midnight.
  Stated in `fleetMetrics.ts`'s own header rather than silently accepted.

### Concepts now genuinely in use
Excluding an undecided case from BOTH sides of a percentage, not just the
numerator — the same shape as "only jobs that finished have a resolution
time" applied to a binary outcome instead of a duration. Reusing one Bayesian
update mechanism for two conceptually different kinds of evidence (a symptom
answer, a vehicle's own history) rather than inventing a second kind of
arithmetic that happens to produce a similar-looking number. Routing two
audiences through one queue by a field that was already there for a smaller
reason (BullMQ's job name), rather than duplicating the debounce-and-deliver
machinery.

### Interview angle
"Two workers read a solver run and both want to escalate a threshold breach —
how does the fleet dashboard avoid double-counting?" It does not read live
state at all — every number is a SUM or a COUNT over rows earlier phases
already committed (`FleetIncident[]`, one query, no lock needed), so there is
no read-then-decide race to have in the first place; the closest thing to one
is two fleet-manager notification rows written by an unordered `Promise.all`,
which is safe because each write is its own row and the whole delivery is
wrapped in one idempotency key.

"Why does a fleet vehicle's prior use the same `applyLikelihood` function a
symptom answer does, instead of its own weighting formula?" Because the two
are the same operation under a different name — "how much should this
observation move the belief" — and reusing the tested, entropy-checkable
mechanism means the prior's effect on question count is provable by the exact
same maths that already governs when intake stops asking, rather than a
second formula nobody has separately verified against `ENTROPY_STOP_THRESHOLD_BITS`.

### What broke
**The fairness grep in `tests/evaluation.test.ts` did not fire this phase —
worth noting why it easily could have.** Phase 20 tripped it once by naming
"earnings Gini" in a comment; Phase 21 does not touch `cost.ts` at all, so the
risk never arose, but it is the second phase in a row where a well-meant
explanatory comment came close to a standing test that greps production
source for forbidden words. Worth remembering before the next comment that
wants to contrast something with fairness.

**The intake-prior end-to-end test polluted its own fixture across manual test
reruns, twice, before landing.** `createIntakeJob` writes a real job with real
`job_events` behind it, and those cannot be deleted (the same append-only rule
`tests/contracts.test.ts` already ran into at Phase 20). The first version of
this test called `createIntakeJob` against `fd-vehicle-a` and `fd-vehicle-repeat`
— vehicles the SAME file also makes exact assertions about ("this vehicle
carries no repeat-pattern flag", "this vehicle carries exactly three
incidents") — and three manual reruns while iterating on the fixture left both
permanently carrying extra FLAT_BATTERY incidents from the debug runs,
eventually flipping a boolean flag on a vehicle the test asserted had none.
Fixed by giving the intake-prior comparison two vehicles dedicated to nothing
else, and by rewriting the one assertion that happened to name a vehicle
positionally ("the flagged vehicle is first") as the general sort invariant it
actually meant ("no flagged vehicle sorts after any unflagged one") — the
second fix is arguably the more durable one, since it no longer depends on
exactly one vehicle in the fixture being flagged.

**Two heredocs sent through the Bash tool failed with `unexpected EOF`, at
the same length threshold Phases 19 and 20 both recorded.** Same fix as
those phases — the Write tool — and the note is now three phases running,
which is enough to stop treating it as a one-off.
